      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2010 Elsevier Ireland Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "PURPOSE",
            "content": "To obtain consistent CCTA image quality and patient dose optimization with an individualized tube current selection method based on analysis of CT scout scans."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS AND MATERIALS",
            "content": "The study received a waiver of informed consent from the institutional review board. Initially 100 patients (Group A) underwent CCTA with a fixed mA. The scout view pixel value and CCTA image noise were measured. Their correlation was studied to establish a formula to determine the required mA for obtaining a CT exam with a specific image noise. One hundred patients (Group B) were then scanned with the formula-determined mA. CCTA image quality, image noise and effective dose from the two groups were statistically analyzed. CT findings for 32 Group B patients were compared with the conventional coronary angiography."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "An average CCTA image noise of 27.6 HU was obtained (target 27 HU) using the formula with more uniform image noise in Group B (standard deviation 2.4 HU) than in Group A (4.1 HU). There was no statistical difference between image quality scores for the two groups. The effective dose for Group B (7.8 mSv) was 30% lower than for Group A (11.2 mSv) (p<0.01). CCTA sensitivity, specificity, positive predictive value, negative predictive value, and stenosis detection accuracy were 94.9%, 92.1%, 88.9%, 96.5% and 93.2%, respectively, for stenosis greater than 50%."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "CCTA mA selection based upon the image characteristics of the CT scout view provides an individualized protocol that generates consistent image quality and helps to reduce overall patient dose."
          }
        ]
      },
